  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 132 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nOverview. \n\nDry and warm weather will continue through Saturday under high \npressure, though skies may appear hazy due to elevated smoke in \naddition to the high clouds. Temperatures and humidity will increase \nsignificantly Sunday into Monday as a heat dome expands eastward. \nThunderstorm complexes will bring rain and storm chances late Sunday \nnight through Monday night, with localized severe weather possible. \nDry weather then is expected through the remainder of the week with \nwarmer than normal temperatures.\n\nTonight Through Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure centered across the eastern Great Lakes will \nmaintain control of central Indiana weather through Saturday, \npromoting broad-scale subsidence and light easterly winds veering to \nsoutheasterly. High-resolution models, including HRRR and RAP smoke \ntransport fields, reveals a persistent plume of elevated wildfire \nsmoke advecting southeastward within upper-level northwest flow \nbeginning late tonight. This bring a return to hazy skies throughout \nSaturday; however, boundary-layer mixing depth and surface \nconcentrations remain low enough to preclude surface visibility or \nair quality impacts.\n\nAs the surface anticyclone shifts toward the Mid-Atlantic Saturday \nnight into Sunday, low-level WAA will intensify. A 850 MB moisture \nplume will surge northward, pushing surface dewpoints into the low \nto mid-70s by Sunday afternoon. Model soundings show steepening mid-\nlevel lapse rates (6.5 to 7.0 C/km) beneath an amplifying upper-\nlevel ridge. High temperatures on Sunday will climb into the upper \n80s to low 90s, yielding afternoon SBCAPE values exceeding 2500-3000 \nJ/kg. High MLCIN and weak synoptic forcing should suppress diurnal \nthunderstorm development through much of the daytime on Sunday, \nkeeping conditions largely cap-inhibited until late evening.\n\nSunday Night Through Friday.\n\nThe synoptic regime through early next week will feature a major \nupper-level heat dome centered over the Central Plains, placing the \nOhio Valley along the eastern periphery in a classic \"ring of fire\" \nnorthwest flow pattern. Guidance from the GFS, ECMWF, and their \nensemble suites (GEFS and EPS) indicates multiple mid-level \nshortwaves traversing the periphery of the ridge, interacting with \nthe warm, unstable boundary layer to trigger organized storm \ncomplexes both Sunday night and Monday night. Monday night currently \nlooks to have the better chance for impacts across central Indiana \nbased on the current axis of the ridge, but that looks to generally \nbe more focused towards northeast Indiana. That being said, there \nmay be a southwestward trend in where the storm complexes may impact \nas the global models often pin the initiation point too far north \nand don't track the progression along the theta-e gradient as well \nas some of the higher resolution models.\n\nHigh DCAPE values exceeding 1000 J/kg and PWAT values near 2.00 \ninches will make damaging wind gusts and localized heavy rainfall \nthe primary hazards with any organized MCS. In addition to the storm \nthreats, heat index values will climb into the 100-105 range Monday \nwhich may need heat headlines in the coming days.\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage early Tuesday, drier continental \nair will briefly build into the Great Lakes, pushing the primary \nthunderstorm boundary south toward the Ohio River. High temperatures \non Tuesday and Wednesday will hover in the upper 80s with reduced \ndewpoints. However, global ensembles agree on sub-tropical ridge \namplification over the Ohio Valley by Thursday and Friday. 500 MB \nheights rising above 590dm, combined with strong solar insolation \nand evapotranspiration, will drive maximum temperatures into the mid-\n90s with heat index values approaching triple digits by the end of \nthe period.",
